<meta type=article>
  <title | Building a Website>
  <author | The Enscribe Project>
</meta>

<config toc toc-location=right />

A **website** is enscribe's third document class, alongside the article and the
book. Where an article is one document and a book is one document read a chapter
at a time, a website is a *set* of documents — many pages, navigated as a site.
Each page is itself an ordinary enscribe document — an article or a book — and the
site is assembled from them by one master document. This page covers writing a
website master and building it to a static site you can deploy anywhere that serves
HTML.

<# The master document #>

A website's entry point is a master with `<meta type=website>` and a `<nav>` that
lists its pages:

```
<meta type=website>
  <title | My Project>
  <icon | logo.svg>
</meta>

<nav>
  <item home | Home>
  <item guide | Guide>
  <nav-group | Reference>
    <item reference/api | API>
    <item reference/cli | CLI>
  </nav-group>
</nav>

<footer src="footer.emd" />
```

The `<nav>` is the **single source of truth for site structure**. An `<item src |
Label>` is a page: `src` points at the page's source (a flat `page.emd`, or a
`page/` directory whose body is `page/index.emd`), and the label is its menu text.
A `<nav-group title="…"> … </nav-group>` groups pages — a dropdown in the top bar,
an expandable node in the sidebar. The brand name and icon in the top bar come from
the master's `<meta>` title and `<icon>`; a site-wide `<footer src>` is set once
here.

Reorganizing the menu is an edit to `<nav>`, never a file move — the same page can
appear anywhere in the menu without moving on disk.

<# Each page is an article or a book #>

A page's source is a normal enscribe document. A page that is `<meta type=article>`
renders as an article; a page that is `<meta type=book>` renders as a book, with
its own chapters and chapter rail. **Nothing is flattened**: a book page keeps book
numbering (a figure stays "2.1"), an article page keeps article numbering ("Figure
1"). The site does not impose a numbering scheme on its pages — each numbers as it
would if you opened it on its own.

<# The build command #>

Point `enscribe build` at the website master and give it an **output directory**:

```
enscribe build site.emd -o site/
```

This writes a **dir-per-page static site**: the first page in the nav is the home
page and lands at the site root (`site/index.html`); every other page is written at
its menu location (`site/guide/index.html`, `site/reference/api/index.html`), so the
URLs mirror the menu (`/guide/`, `/reference/api/`). A page that is itself a book is
published as its own set of per-chapter pages, nested under the page's directory.

Because a site is many files, `-o` must be a **directory**. The pretty
trailing-slash URLs need an HTTP server (the trailing slash resolves to `index.html`
via the host's directory index); a website is not meant to be opened off `file://`.

<# Linking between pages #>

Within the site, link to another page by its **slug** with `<a {slug} | label>`:

```
See <a guide | the guide> to get started.
```

The builder resolves the slug to the target page's URL, relative to the current
page's depth — so a link keeps working when you reorganize the menu. A page's slug
is its stable identity, taken from the first of: an explicit `<meta slug="…">` in
the page, else its `<meta title>`, else its nav label. (Pin a `<meta slug>` on a
page you link to, so renaming its title can't break inbound links.) A link to a
slug no page has does not stop the build — it renders the link text in place,
flagged, with a warning on the console.

**Cross-references reach across pages too.** A `<ref @id>` to a figure, table, or
section on another page resolves to that target's own number and links to the page
it lives on — in every direction, including a reference from an article page into a
book page's chapter (which shows the book-native "Figure 2.1").

<# Reading it live #>

The same master also builds a **live** site — a portable folder whose pages render
in the browser, navigated by a client-side `?page=slug` router:

```
enscribe build site.emd --live -o site/
```

Serve that folder over HTTP and the site renders client-side, with the back/forward
buttons working. The static build (above) is the deployable, search-engine-friendly
projection of the same site.
